# 4. Interrogating a run

The first three tutorials wrote Turbulance and read the output block below the
cell. This one is about the other three commands: the ones that let you ask what
actually happened, rather than trusting the printed answer.

| command | the question it answers |
|---|---|
| ndombolo trace | what happened, event by event, in order |
| ndombolo graph | what the script declares, and what touches what |
| ndombolo record | what runs have ever happened to this file |

All three take a .ndo file and print JSON. None of them writes to the document.

Every claim below was checked against the code and against a real run. Where a
surface behaves unlike the obvious guess, the guess is named rather than
smoothed over.

## A document worth interrogating

Two small cells, so the trace stays short enough to read in full.

```turbulance
item base = 10
item scale = 3
item total = base * scale
print("total is", total)
```

```output
total is 30

base = 10
scale = 3
total = 30
```


The second cell uses `total` from the first, so there is something for the
graph to link across a cell boundary.

```turbulance
item labels = ["base", "scale"]
item n = len(labels)
item summary = total + n
print(summary)
```

```output
32

labels = [base, scale]
n = 2
summary = 32
```


Run the document first. Every command below reads a run, and `record` in
particular has nothing to show until one has happened.

    ndombolo run 04-interrogating-a-run.ndo

## `trace`: what happened, in order

    ndombolo trace 04-interrogating-a-run.ndo

One object with a count and a list:

    {
      "events": 10,
      "trace": [
        { "cell": 0, "event": { ... } },
        ...
      ]
    }

Each event carries a `seq` that counts across the whole run rather than
restarting per cell, a `rule` naming what fired, a `site` with the line,
and then `reads` plus either `writes` or `note`.

### `reads` is not a list of names

This is the part worth reading slowly, because the obvious guess is wrong.
`reads` is a list of records, and each one carries the value the item held at
the moment it was read:

    "reads": [
      { "item": "base",  "value": 10 },
      { "item": "scale", "value": 3 }
    ]

That is what makes the trace an answer rather than a hint. A bare name would
send you back to the script to work out what it held; the value is already
there.

### Builtins appear as reads

Looking up `len` is a read like any other, so it appears in the list:

    { "item": "len", "value": { "builtin": "_len" } }

So `item n = len(labels)` reads two things, not one. If you count reads expecting only
your own items, the builtin will look like an extra.

### `print` is two events, not one

A printing statement emits a `print` event and then an `expr` event, and
they carry different halves of the story:

- the `print` event has the printed line in its `note` and an empty
  `reads`;
- the enclosing `expr` event carries the reads, and its `note` is the
  string `"None"`. That is a string, not JSON null, and it is the value the
  statement evaluated to.

So if you want to know what a printed line depended on, look at the `expr`
beside it. The `print` itself will tell you nothing.

### `writes`

    "writes": { "item": "total", "value": 30, "tag": "num" }

The tag is the value kind. That matters because Turbulance compares `1` and
`true` as equal, so the tag is the only thing in the trace that tells them
apart.

### It stops at the first failing cell

If a cell fails, `trace` reports the events up to and including that cell and
stops. Later cells did not run, so they have no events to report. `graph`
behaves differently, and the difference is deliberate.

## `graph`: what the script declares

    ndombolo graph 04-interrogating-a-run.ndo

Two lists. `items` is every declared name with the line it was declared at
and the `stage`, meaning the cell index, it was declared in. `contacts` is
the directed edges between them, with a weight.

    {
      "items": [
        { "name": "base",  "kind": "item", "line": 24, "stage": 0 },
        ...
      ],
      "contacts": [
        { "from": "base", "to": "total", "weight": 1 },
        ...
      ],
      "item_count": 6,
      "contact_count": 5
    }

This is a static reading. It parses the cells; it does not run them. That is why
a cell which does not parse is skipped rather than fatal: the indices come back
under `cells_that_did_not_parse` so you know the picture is partial. A half-written cell
should not stop you seeing what the rest of the document declares.

### Four things it silently does not do

Each of these is deliberate, and each will look like a bug the first time you
meet it.

A name used twice in one expression is one contact. `item b = a + a` gives the edge
from `a` to `b` a weight of 1, not 2. Free names are collected as a set.

A redeclaration keeps the first site. Declare `a` in cell 0 and again in cell
1, and the graph still reports the line and stage from cell 0. There is no
second entry. Keeping the later one would break the guarantee that the graph
after n cells contains the graph after n minus one.

An edge with an undeclared endpoint vanishes. `len` is not a declared item,
so `item d = len(c)` yields `c` to `d` and nothing from `len`. The graph is
over the document's own names.

A self-contact is dropped, but the declaration is not. `item x = x` produces no
edge. It still produces the item: `x` appears in `items` with its line.
Only the loop is gone. This is the one most likely to be misread as the whole
statement being ignored.

### It accretes

Adding a cell extends the graph. Nothing is ever removed and no weight ever goes
down. That is the same discipline the record keeps, and it is why an undo in the
editor advances the record instead of rewinding it.

## `record`: what has ever happened

    ndombolo record 04-interrogating-a-run.ndo

    {
      "record": 4,
      "file": "04-interrogating-a-run.ndo.record",
      "deposits": [
        { "record": 1, "record_before": 0, "cell": 0, "events": 5,
          "items_touched": ["base", "scale", "total"],
          "completed": true, "at": 1788196481 },
        ...
      ]
    }

It lives in a file beside the document, so `foo.ndo` keeps `foo.ndo.record`, as one
JSON object per line, appended and never rewritten. A line-oriented format is
the point: appending cannot corrupt what is already there, and a truncated final
line costs one deposit rather than the file.

`record_before` is there so the counter is auditable rather than merely
asserted. Each deposit says what the count was before it and what it is after,
so a gap would be visible.

### The record is not the document

This is the distinction the whole design turns on, and the easiest way to see it
is to do it.

Run the document twice. The output blocks look identical, because an output
block is a view of the last run, replaced each time; a cell run ten times leaves
one block. The record, meanwhile, went up by the number of cells, twice.

Now clear the outputs:

    ndombolo clear 04-interrogating-a-run.ndo

It prints something like `cleared 2 output blocks, record still 4`. The views are gone from the file; the
deposits are untouched. Clearing removes what you can see, not what happened.

### Every run deposits, including the ones you discard

Two cases make this concrete.

`ndombolo run f.ndo --cell 1` writes only the output block of cell 1. But cell 0 still ran. It
has to, because the meaning of cell 1 depends on the store cell 0 left. So the
record advances by two, not one. The flag selects what is written, not what is
run.

And a cell that fails deposits too, with `completed` false. A run that raised
is still a run that happened.

## One footnote on line numbers

`ndombolo cells` prints the fence line of each cell. `trace` and `graph`
print the line of the first line of source, one further down. So a cell listed
at line 23 by `ndombolo cells` will have its first event at line 24. They are not
disagreeing; they are naming different lines.

Lines also move as you run and clear. An output block is part of the file, so
inserting one pushes every later cell down; clearing pulls it back up. Cell 1
here reports line 35 in a cleared document and line 43 in a run one. The line
is where the cell is now, not a name it keeps.

## Why there is no cache

Every one of these commands replays the document from the top through a fresh
session. So does the ask panel in the editor. Nothing stores the store from the
previous run to resume from, and nothing remembers a previous answer so that a
repeated question could skip the search.

That is not an oversight waiting to be optimised. A determination has to be
reached by search each time; a lookup table of past determinations is exactly
what the design forbids. Probing is cheap and you may probe as often as you
like. What you may not do is remember the verdict instead of re-reaching it.

## Things to try

- Trace the document and find the event that wrote `total`. Its `reads`
  give you both operands and their values.
- Find the `expr` event beside the first `print`. Note that the
  `print` holds the text and the `expr` holds the dependencies.
- Graph it and check that `summary` has two in-edges, and that `len`
  appears nowhere.
- Run it again, then `clear`, then `record`. Count the deposits and
  compare against the number of output blocks left in the file.
- Add a cell containing a syntax error and run `graph` again: the rest of the
  document still graphs, and the broken cell is named rather than fatal.
- Add `item base = 99` to the second cell and graph it. The item still reports line
  24, stage 0.

The next tutorial takes up the evidential layer: propositions, motions, and the
verdicts that contact them.
